# SpeakHired — Speak well. Get hired.

Free, open-source voice coach for IT professionals who want to speak confident English and pass interviews. It runs on your own computer in one of two modes: **Offline** (AI on your machine) or **Online** (a fast cloud AI with your own key). In both, your voice and grammar checks stay on your computer, and your name, contact details and IDs are removed before any AI sees your text.

- **Interview practice**: paste a job description (or link) and upload your resume. A UK-accented interviewer asks questions out loud, round by round (1 screening to 4 system design), then corrects your answer and shows a better way to say it.
- **Hot Seat**: rehearse one to three real questions you were asked, with a web-checked model answer.
- **Clean Copy**: a Grammarly-style checker for cover letters and recruiter emails.
- **Resume Radar**: an ATS-readiness score for your resume, with fixes.
- **Daily English for IT**: terms, jargon and professional phrases (coming next).

## How it works
An AI model writes questions and feedback: **Ollama** on your computer (Offline) or **Groq/Grok** with your key (Online). [LEV](https://huggingface.co/interfaze-ai/lev) makes the scoring decisions (on your computer, your network or your own Modal server). faster-whisper listens, Kokoro speaks. See "Offline and Online mode" below for the details and the privacy rules.

## Which models it uses
| Job | Model | Where it runs |
|---|---|---|
| Offline mode (questions, feedback) | `qwen3:4b` through Ollama (`OLLAMA_MODEL` in `.env`) | Your computer |
| Online mode (the same jobs) | `openai/gpt-oss-120b` on Groq, or `grok-4.7` on xAI (`EXTERNAL_MODEL` in `.env`) | The service's cloud |
| Scoring and decisions | LEV (based on Qwen3.5-4B) | Your Modal server, or your own computer or network |
| Listening to you | Whisper `small` (`WHISPER_MODEL` in `.env`) | Your computer |
| Interviewer voice | Kokoro (British voices) | Your computer |
| Grammar check in Clean Copy | Harper | Your browser |

In Online mode only the AI step moves to the cloud, and your name, contact details and IDs are removed first. Listening, speaking and the grammar check always stay on your computer.

## Run it

### Step 0: choose a mode
| | **Offline** | **Online** |
|---|---|---|
| AI answers come from | Ollama on your computer | Groq or Grok, with your own free key |
| Needs | Ollama, about 3 GB of disk for the model, 16 GB RAM to feel quick | A key and an internet connection |
| Best for | Privacy, no internet, no usage limits | Small computers (8 GB): answers in seconds, not minutes |
| Install with | `./scripts/setup.sh` | `./scripts/setup.sh --online` |

You can switch any time with the toggle at the top right of the app, so it is fine to set up both. Not sure? Run `uv run python -m speakhired.doctor` after installing: it checks your computer and suggests a mode.

### Step 1: install the tools (once)
| Tool | macOS | Windows | Linux | Needed for |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/) (Python manager) | `brew install uv` | `winget install astral-sh.uv` | `curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh \| sh` | Both |
| [Node.js 20+](https://nodejs.org) | `brew install node` | `winget install OpenJS.NodeJS.LTS` | your package manager | Both |
| espeak-ng (for the Kokoro voice) | `brew install espeak-ng` | [installer](https://github.com/espeak-ng/espeak-ng/releases) | `sudo apt install espeak-ng` | Both |
| [Ollama](https://ollama.com/download) | `brew install ollama` or the app | installer from ollama.com | `curl -fsSL https://ollama.com/install.sh \| sh` | **Offline only** |

### Step 2: get the code and your settings file
```bash
git clone <this repo> && cd speakhired
cp .env.example .env
```

### Step 3A: Offline setup (Ollama)
The Ollama desktop app and the Windows installer start the server for you. With the Homebrew or Linux install, start it yourself and leave that terminal open:
```bash
ollama serve
```
Then install the project; this also downloads the model (about 2.5 GB, once):
```bash
./scripts/setup.sh          # Windows: .\scripts\setup.ps1
```
If `ollama serve` says "address already in use", Ollama is already running. That is fine.

### Step 3B: Online setup (Groq or Grok, no Ollama needed)
1. Get a key. **Groq** (free tier, recommended): sign in at [console.groq.com](https://console.groq.com), open **API Keys**, create one. **Grok from xAI**: [console.x.ai](https://console.x.ai).
2. Install the project:
   ```bash
   ./scripts/setup.sh --online          # Windows: .\scripts\setup.ps1 -Online
   ```
3. Open `.env` and paste the key:
   ```
   EXTERNAL_API_KEY=your-key-here
   ```
   `.env.example` already holds the matching address and model for Groq. For Grok instead, use the Grok lines shown in the comments of that file. The key and the address must come from the same company (Groq keys start `gsk_`, xAI keys start `xai-`).

Without a key the app still starts; choosing Online then shows a "key is not configured" notice for 10 seconds and tells you what to fix.

### Step 4: LEV (the scoring model), optional but recommended
LEV works in both modes. See "LEV: the scoring model" below for running it on your computer or on Modal. Without it, the AI model scores answers itself.

### Step 5: start it
```bash
./scripts/run.sh            # Windows: .\scripts\run.ps1
```
Open **http://127.0.0.1:5173**. The app remembers your last mode; switch with the toggle at the top right, or from the "How it runs" cards on the Home page.

### Using it
- Press **Space** or the mic button to start answering, and again to finish. Check the transcript, fix anything misheard, then press **Get feedback**.
- First-time waits: the first spoken answer downloads the Whisper model (about 500 MB), preparing an interview takes about a minute, and the first LEV score on Modal can take about a minute while it wakes up.
- Stop the app with Ctrl+C in the terminal.

### Check that it works
Open http://127.0.0.1:8100/api/health. You want `ollama.ok: true`, `model_ready: true` and `lev.ok: true`. You can also run `uv run python -m speakhired.doctor`.

### Quick try without speech models
Install with `uv sync --extra dev` instead of `./scripts/setup.sh`. Questions are then spoken by your browser's UK voice and you type your answers.

### Troubleshooting
| Problem | Fix |
|---|---|
| `zsh: no such file or directory: ./scripts/setup.sh` | You are not in the project folder. `cd` into `speakhired` first (put quotes around paths that contain spaces). |
| `could not connect to ollama server` | The server is not running. Start it with `ollama serve` (or open the Ollama app) and try again. |
| `ollama serve`: `address already in use` | Ollama is already running. Nothing to fix. |
| `ollama: command not found` | Install Ollama (step 1), then open a new terminal. |
| Health shows `ollama.ok: false` | Start Ollama, and check `OLLAMA_URL` in `.env` (default `http://localhost:11434`). |
| Health shows `model_ready: false` | Run `ollama pull qwen3:4b`, or set `OLLAMA_MODEL` in `.env` to a model shown by `ollama list`. |
| Questions take very long, or the Mac freezes | Close other apps. Try `WHISPER_MODEL=base` in `.env`. On 8 GB, keep LEV on Modal rather than local. |
| `No module named 'faster_whisper'` / `'kokoro'` / `'soundfile'` (voice not installed) | Run `uv sync --extra voice --extra dev` from the project folder, then restart. The app shows "Install voice extras" in the browser until you do. |
| Kokoro install or voice fails (macOS or Linux) | Install `espeak-ng` (step 1) and run `uv sync --extra voice --extra dev` again. |
| Interviewer uses a robotic browser voice | The Kokoro voice is not installed. Use the fix on the line above. |
| `Couldn't access the microphone` | Allow the microphone for the page in your browser settings, or type your answer. |
| `TypeError: open() got an unexpected keyword argument 'metadata_errors'` when you speak | Your `av` package is too new. Run `uv sync --extra voice --extra dev` (it pins `av` below 19) and restart. |
| Transcription is slow the first time | It is downloading the Whisper model once. Wait, or use `WHISPER_MODEL=base`. |
| Health shows `lev.ok: false` | Check `LEV_URL`, `MODAL_KEY`, `MODAL_SECRET` and `LEV_ALLOW_REMOTE=true` in `.env`, wait a minute if Modal is waking up, or set `LEV_MODE=off`. |
| `remote LEV hosts need LEV_ALLOW_REMOTE=true and https` | Add `LEV_ALLOW_REMOTE=true` to `.env` (only if you trust that LEV server), or use a local LEV. |
| Port already in use (5173 or 8100) | Stop the old run with Ctrl+C, or `pkill -f uvicorn` and `pkill -f vite`. |
| Changed `.env` but nothing changed | Stop and start `./scripts/run.sh` again. |

## LEV: the scoring model
LEV decides how well you answered (relevance, correctness, depth, structure, examples) and what the interviewer does next. It is a classifier by [InterfazeAI](https://github.com/InterfazeAI/lev) (Apache-2.0), about 8 GB, and runs as a small server that SpeakHired calls over HTTP (`lev-http`). Choose one option.

Setup first: `cp .env.example .env`, then edit `.env`.

### Option A: run LEV offline on your computer (private, recommended if you have 16 GB+ RAM)
```bash
uv tool install "lev[serve] @ git+https://github.com/InterfazeAI/lev#subdirectory=packages/lev"
lev serve --checkpoint interfaze-ai/lev --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8000
```
The first start downloads about 8 GB of weights from Hugging Face; after that it works with no internet (`HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1`). Keep this running in its own terminal. In `.env`:
```
LEV_MODE=http
LEV_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8000
LEV_ALLOW_REMOTE=false
```
Tip: you can run this on another computer you own on your home or college network (use `--host 0.0.0.0` and set `LEV_URL=http://<its-ip>:8000`).

### Option B: run LEV on Modal (for computers with less than 16 GB)
Modal has a free monthly credit; check its current pricing. You deploy LEV once under your own account:
```bash
pip install modal && modal setup
modal deploy deploy/modal_lev.py
```
Then in the Modal dashboard create a **Proxy Auth Token** and fill `.env`:
```
LEV_MODE=http
LEV_URL=https://<your-workspace>--lev-server-serve.modal.run
MODAL_KEY=wk-...
MODAL_SECRET=ws-...
LEV_ALLOW_REMOTE=true
```
Like Online mode, this sends data off your computer: the interview question, its key points and your **redacted** answer (emails, phone numbers and links removed; no resume content) go to your own Modal app. The first request after idle can take a minute while the server wakes up. Never commit `.env` or share your keys.

### Option C: no LEV
Set `LEV_MODE=off`. Ollama scores answers instead, which works but is less consistent.

Check which one is active: open `http://127.0.0.1:8100/api/health`.

## Hot Seat: rehearse one or two real questions
Paste 1 to 3 questions from a real interview or the internet (optionally add your rough idea for each). The interviewer asks them aloud, you answer by voice, and you get a score, grammar fixes, **"Instead of this, say this"** swaps, and a model answer in four parts (answer first, example, trade-off, close).

**Web check (optional, off by default).** Add a free key for any OpenAI-compatible service (xAI Grok, Groq, OpenRouter...) to `.env` as `EXTERNAL_API_KEY` (and `EXTERNAL_API_BASE` / `EXTERNAL_MODEL` if not using Grok), then switch on "Check my answer on the web" in Hot Seat. It looks up what a strong answer should cover and flags wrong claims. What is sent:
- **The question** (emails, phone numbers and links removed, 400 characters at most).
- **Your answer and idea only if you also switch on "Include my answer in the web check"** (same clean-up, 1,500 characters at most).
- Never your resume, job description or audio. A "What will be sent" panel shows the exact text, and every call is logged on your computer in `data/egress_audit.jsonl`.

Without a key, Hot Seat still works fully offline; the result just isn't web-checked. Source links come from the AI search tool, so open them with care.

## Offline and Online mode
Use the switch at the top right of the app.

| | Offline (default) | Online |
|---|---|---|
| Who writes the AI answers | Ollama on this computer | Groq or Grok, using your own key |
| Reasoning and scoring | LEV (local, your LAN, or your Modal server) | LEV on your Modal server, plus Groq or Grok |
| Good for | Privacy, no internet, no cost | Small computers: it is much faster and uses almost no memory |

The key, address and model for Online mode (set in `.env`, then restart):
- **Groq** ([console.groq.com](https://console.groq.com), free tier, key starts `gsk_`): `EXTERNAL_API_BASE=https://api.groq.com/openai/v1` and `EXTERNAL_MODEL=openai/gpt-oss-120b`
- **Grok from xAI** ([console.x.ai](https://console.x.ai), key starts `xai-`): `EXTERNAL_API_BASE=https://api.x.ai/v1` and `EXTERNAL_MODEL=grok-4.7`

Groq and Grok are different companies. The key and the address must match, and the app tells you if they don't. If the key is missing or the service can't be reached, a notice tile appears for 10 seconds and the app tells you what to fix. It never silently falls back to the other mode. Your choice is remembered.

**What stays where.** The Home page tells you which of these you are in:

| Setup | Writing the answers | Scoring (LEV) | Leaves your computer |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Fully local**: Offline mode, `lev serve` on this computer (`LEV_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8000`), or LEV off | Ollama, on this computer | This computer | Nothing |
| Offline mode, LEV on another computer in your home or college network | Ollama, on this computer | Your network | Your answers, personal details removed, to that computer |
| Offline mode, LEV on your Modal server | Ollama, on this computer | The internet (your Modal app) | Your answers, personal details removed, to your Modal app |
| Online mode | Groq or Grok | Wherever LEV runs, as above | Text with personal details removed, to the cloud AI (and to LEV if it is remote) |

The app works out where LEV runs from `LEV_URL`: `localhost` or `127.0.0.1` means this computer, a home or office network address (such as `192.168.x.x`) means your network, and anything else means the internet.

**Personal details never reach any AI, in either mode.** Before any text goes to Ollama, Groq, Grok or LEV, the app replaces your name, email, phone number, links and social handles, addresses, birth date, Aadhaar, PAN, passport and other ID numbers, bank and card numbers, and personal fields such as marital status with placeholders like `[NAME_1]`. The answer you see has the real text put back. Employers, colleges, cities and technologies are kept because a review needs them. The resume report and interview setup list what was removed (counts only, never the values).

Names are found from the first line of a resume, "Name:" fields, titles such as Mr, Ms and Dr, and phrases like "worked with Ravi Kumar". A name tucked into the middle of a sentence with none of those signs can slip through. To see exactly what the AI would receive from your own file, run this (it works entirely on your computer):
```bash
uv run python -m speakhired.redact_preview path/to/your-resume.pdf
```

## If Offline mode feels slow
Offline speed depends on your computer's memory far more than its processor. On an 8 GB machine that is also running a browser and an editor, the model gets squeezed into slow "swap" memory and everything crawls. In order of effect:
1. **Close other apps and browser tabs**, then check with `uv run python -m speakhired.doctor --bench` (about 30 seconds). It reports the speed, how much slow swap memory is in use, and the waits to expect.
2. **Use a smaller model.** `ollama pull qwen3:1.7b`, then set `OLLAMA_MODEL=qwen3:1.7b` in `.env` and restart. It is roughly twice as fast; its answers are simpler. Run the benchmark again to compare: `uv run python -m speakhired.doctor --bench qwen3:1.7b`.
3. **Switch to Online mode** (toggle at the top right) when you want answers in seconds.
4. In **Resume Radar**, untick "Use AI to review the content" for an instant score from clear rules.

What the app already does for you: it turns off the model's hidden "thinking" (this alone cut one feedback from about 76 s to about 31 s in testing), keeps the model loaded for 30 minutes (`OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE`), caps runaway answers (`OLLAMA_MAX_TOKENS`), and splits the feedback in two so the corrected answer and the points to add appear first while "the answer to give the interviewer" is still being written.

## Technical words, spoken and heard
Interviews are full of words that voices and recognisers get wrong (ChatGPT, Claude, kubectl, nginx, PostgreSQL, OAuth, GPT-4). The app keeps one dictionary of about 150 of them in `apps/api/speakhired/data/termbank.json` and uses it two ways:
- **When the interviewer speaks**, "ChatGPT" is said as "Chat G P T", "kubectl" as "cube control", and symbols and special dashes that confuse voices are cleaned up. The voice also pauses between sentences and runs slightly slower than normal. Change the pace with the **Voice** menu on the interview screen. If the main voice is unavailable, the browser's British voice reads the same wording.
- **When you speak**, the recogniser is told which technical words to expect (the question, the job's tech stack, a list of popular terms), and anything still misheard ("chat gpt", "post gress", "cooper netties") is corrected to the standard spelling. A note under the answer box shows what was fixed.

To add a word, add an entry to `termbank.json`: the correct spelling, how it should be said (`say`), and spellings recognisers tend to produce (`heard`). `uv run pytest` checks that ordinary sentences are never changed.

## English-only practice
This app is for practising English, so it tells you when you slip into another language. It checks three things: the language Whisper hears in your audio, whether the text contains Indian-language scripts (Devanagari, Telugu, Tamil and others), and a word list of Indian-language words written in English letters ("mujhe", "kya", "ledu", "enna").
- A few stray words: you get a note, the words are highlighted, and the English alternative is shown ("matlab" becomes "I mean").
- A mostly non-English answer: you are asked to re-record in English before getting feedback.

The word list is `apps/api/speakhired/data/indic_words.json` (Hindi, Telugu, Tamil and Kannada so far). Contributions are welcome: add only words that are not also English words, and run `uv run pytest` to check that ordinary English sentences are never flagged.

## The interface
A clean white-and-blue design with a dark mode (sun/moon button, top right). It is built with [Tailwind CSS](https://tailwindcss.com) and [daisyUI](https://daisyui.com) (both MIT), with the Fraunces and Plus Jakarta Sans fonts (SIL Open Font License) bundled into the app, so nothing is loaded from the internet.

## Status
Interview flow, scoring, coaching, privacy gateway and UI are in place. Not yet built: Daily English module, automatic end-of-speech detection (you press Space to finish), pronunciation scoring, LEV `local` in-process mode.

## Tests
```bash
uv run pytest
```
